export const debate = {"id":"horn-bertuzzi-oconnor-schmid-problem-evil-2022","number":"115","title":"Trent Horn and Cameron Bertuzzi vs Alex O'Connor and Joe Schmid: Problem of Evil","label":"Panel problem of evil","date":"2026-07-28","duration":"1 hr 15 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hxUgziZ4vro","motion":"Does suffering, especially animal suffering and apparently gratuitous evil, make perfect-being theism improbable, or can theodicy, skeptical theism, higher goods, and Christian hope answer the challenge?","summary":"Horn and Bertuzzi defend theodicy and Christian hope; O'Connor and Schmid press evidential evil, animal suffering, and the cost of skeptical replies.","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"certain goods you can't have them without a concomitant evil","context":"The theist side's central strategy is that some valuable goods, including courage, compassion, defeat of evil, and final restoration, may require tolerated evils."},"con":{"text":"hundreds of millions of years of this kind of evil","context":"The atheist/agnostic side's central pressure is that evolutionary animal suffering looks expected on indifference but surprising on perfect-being theism."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Catholic-Protestant theodicy","speaker":"Trent Horn and Cameron Bertuzzi","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Atheist-agnostic evil critique","speaker":"Alex O'Connor and Joe Schmid","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":78,"con":84},"sections":[{"title":"Power And Higher Goods","timebox":"01:17-16:15","score":{"pro":79,"con":82},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"time":"08:55","role":"Omnipotence limit","words":"The theist side says omnipotence is not compromised if no amount of power can realize goods without certain evils.","score":79,"critique":"The omnipotence move is useful because it prevents a crude version of the problem of evil from demanding contradictions. If free will, courage, compassion, or other goods really cannot exist without some risk, fear, or suffering, then God's inability to separate them would not be a defect in power. The argument also distinguishes perfect-being theism from a weaker God who merely wants to help but cannot. The weakness is that the key modal premise is asserted more than shown. Listeners are asked to accept that important goods require evils of the actual kind and scale. The score is solid because the distinction is legitimate, but it does not yet justify this world's suffering.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"06:03","role":"Theodicy pressure","words":"O'Connor says many theodicies make God unable to prevent suffering because some desired good supposedly requires it.","score":82,"critique":"O'Connor's pressure on theodicy is strong because it translates familiar defenses into a shared structure. Whether the good is free will, courage, or higher-order virtue, the explanation often says God has a valuable aim that cannot be realized without suffering. That exposes how much turns on the alleged necessity relation. The weakness is that he risks flattening important distinctions among logical impossibility, divine obligation, and voluntarily chosen means. A free-will defense and a soul-making defense do not all have the same burden. Still, the move usefully asks whether theodicies are simply relocating limits into the goods God wants. The score is high because the structure is clear and relevant.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"time":"12:22","role":"Axiology stalemate","words":"Bertuzzi says the dispute may bottom out in whether a world with higher-order goods and accompanying evils is more valuable.","score":78,"critique":"Bertuzzi's axiology-stalemate point is fair and disarming. Some disputes about evil really do depend on basic judgments about what kinds of goods are worth having, and he is right that philosophy often reaches competing seemings. Naming that limit keeps the panel from pretending that every theodicy can be settled by quick examples. The weakness is that stalemate language can arrive too early. If one side says racism, cancer, or predation is not worth the virtues it makes possible, the theist still owes more than a contrary intuition. The score is solid because the meta-point is honest, but it can function as a brake on needed argument.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"14:50","role":"Parasitic-goods challenge","words":"O'Connor says we would prefer no racism and no need for Martin Luther King over racism plus heroic resistance.","score":84,"critique":"O'Connor's Martin Luther King example is an effective intuitive test of higher-order goods. Heroic resistance is valuable, but it seems parasitic on a prior evil that we would rather never existed. The analogy makes theodicy emotionally and morally concrete without requiring technical premises. The weakness is that it may not cover every proposed good. Compassion, forgiveness, freely chosen love, or defeated evil may not function exactly like bravery under danger, and theists can contest whether final restoration changes the value calculation. Still, the example identifies a serious problem for simple soul-making appeals. The score is high because it clarifies why not every virtue justifies its enabling evil.","tags":[]}}]},{"title":"Logical Or Evidential","timebox":"16:17-25:53","score":{"pro":80,"con":84},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"time":"21:29","role":"Threshold distinction","words":"Horn distinguishes any evil disproving God from particular quantities or qualities of evil making God unlikely.","score":80,"critique":"Horn's threshold distinction is useful because it identifies a real difference between all-or-none incompatibility and evidence from kinds or quantities of suffering. If an atheist grants that some suffering could be justified, the theist can fairly ask why similar defenses cannot extend to harder cases. That is a legitimate pressure point, especially for animal suffering or extreme evils framed as probabilistic evidence. The weakness is that the distinction can look overly tidy. O'Connor is right that a deductively valid argument can focus on a specified range of facts rather than any evil whatsoever. The score is strong because Horn clarifies a real dialectical issue, but it does not dissolve the evidential argument.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"16:29","role":"Category collapse","words":"O'Connor says the logical/evidential distinction can be used rhetorically to weaken evil arguments unfairly.","score":84,"critique":"O'Connor's criticism of the logical/evidential distinction is strong because it exposes a debate tactic: treat a deductive evil argument as dead unless every premise is logically necessary, while allowing other deductive arguments to rest on defended but contestable premises. His Kalam comparison usefully shows why that standard would be odd if applied consistently. The weakness is that he initially seems to use logical in a broader sense than the traditional any-evil incompatibility argument, which creates some semantic friction. Once he clarifies that a logical argument can target a specified evil, the point improves. The score is high because the objection protects evil arguments from being dismissed by label management.","tags":[]}}]},{"title":"Animal Suffering","timebox":"25:54-42:22","score":{"pro":75,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"time":"30:14","role":"Animal afterlife","words":"Horn says Catholic options include animals suffering less than assumed or being compensated with endless fulfillment.","score":75,"critique":"Horn's survey of animal-suffering replies is helpful because it shows the theist has more than one possible route. He distinguishes minimizing animal suffering, denying moral blameworthiness, and compensating conscious animals through fulfillment or resurrection. That keeps the problem from being treated as if no Christian philosopher has noticed it. The weakness is that the answers feel speculative at exactly the point where the suffering is vivid. An animal afterlife or cognitive boost may be theologically allowed, but the panel gives little reason to think it is true. Minimizing animal suffering also risks underplaying visible distress, predation, and disease. The score is solid because the options are real, but their evidential support is thin.","tags":[{"label":"Scope neglect","type":"bias","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/scope-neglect/","context":"The reply can reduce the perceived force of animal suffering by emphasizing possible compensation or uncertain intensity."}]},"con":{"time":"25:54","role":"Evolutionary suffering","words":"Schmid says natural selection builds predation, parasitism, death, and hundreds of millions of years of animal suffering into creation.","score":88,"critique":"Schmid's animal-suffering argument is the strongest evidential case in the panel. He frames it probabilistically: a world where natural reality is indifferent predicts predation, disease, parasitism, and vast suffering more readily than perfect-being theism does. The koala fire example makes the scale concrete without becoming the whole argument. The strength is that animal suffering bypasses many human free-will defenses and focuses on the means by which life developed. The weakness is that the argument depends on comparative expectations about what God would choose, which skeptical theists dispute. Still, the burden lands hard: evolutionary suffering is not a marginal blemish but woven into the creative process. The score is very high.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"time":"32:55","role":"Pleasure balance","words":"Horn warns against describing evolutionary history as only a gore-fest, since animal lives may include pleasure and neutral experience.","score":74,"critique":"Horn's balance warning has some value because rhetoric about nature can selectively spotlight horror. Animal lives include feeding, play, bonding, rest, mating, and neutral states, not only pain. That matters if the argument implicitly assumes existence is net negative. The weakness is that the problem of evil does not require animal lives to be only suffering. Apparently gratuitous terror, starvation, disease, and predation can still be powerful evidence even if much animal life is neutral or pleasant. Horn's thought experiment about seeding life on another planet is interesting but not fully parallel to an omnipotent creator with many possible methods. The score is mixed-solid because the caution helps, but it does not blunt the central scale problem.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"35:10","role":"Author-of-rules reply","words":"O'Connor says God is not stuck inside nature's rules; God could reduce suffering without destroying animal life.","score":87,"critique":"O'Connor's author-of-rules reply is highly effective because it rejects an analogy that treats humans and God as equally embedded in nature. Humans deciding whether to destroy the Amazon face tradeoffs inside an existing system; an omnipotent creator allegedly authored the system and its biological rules. That distinction makes the button analogy much sharper: God could create animals with less suffering, different pain thresholds, or different psychophysical laws. The weakness is that possible redesigns need more detail to show they preserve the goods the theist values, including agency, ecological stability, and real animal life. Even so, the reply squarely targets the divine-design burden. The score is very high because it answers the analogy directly.","tags":[]}}]},{"title":"Skeptical Theism","timebox":"42:29-50:24","score":{"pro":78,"con":84},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"time":"47:15","role":"Heroic-good reply","words":"Horn says skeptical theism need not paralyze us because the unknown good might be our stopping the suffering.","score":78,"critique":"Horn's reply to moral paralysis is one of the better defenses of skeptical theism. If our knowledge is limited, then we also cannot rule out that the relevant good is precisely our intervention, rescue, or obedience to a known moral command. That blocks the easy conclusion that mysterious divine reasons should make us passive. The weakness is that this answer mainly saves everyday moral action; it does not fully restore our ability to assess God's permission of evils. If our sample of goods and evils is deeply unrepresentative, many natural-theology predictions may still weaken. The score is solid because the paralysis objection is answered locally, but skeptical theism remains costly.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"42:57","role":"Universal-acid worry","words":"O'Connor and Schmid warn that if God's reasons are too hidden, skeptical theism can erode natural theology and moral inference.","score":84,"critique":"The universal-acid worry is strong because it targets the cost of skeptical theism rather than merely mocking mystery. If humans are too cognitively limited to judge whether horrific suffering is probable on theism, then it becomes harder to run fine-tuning, design, or providence arguments that require predictions about what God would do. O'Connor's drowning-child example also gives the worry moral teeth. The weakness is that the strongest forms of skeptical theism can combine revealed duties, local moral knowledge, and more cautious probability claims. That prevents a total collapse into paralysis. Still, the critique identifies a real tradeoff: hiding reasons for evil may also hide expected divine behavior. The score is high.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"time":"48:02","role":"Cumulative weighing","words":"Horn says easy solutions fail, but evil must be weighed against independent reasons for God.","score":80,"critique":"Horn's cumulative-weighing point is methodologically sound. He concedes that easy answers to evil are suspect, then insists that evidence from evil should be weighed against whatever independent arguments one has for God. That is exactly how broad worldview disputes usually work. The weakness is that the move can become too generic unless the competing evidence is specified and compared. A listener cannot know how much evil lowers the probability of perfect-being theism unless the alleged positive evidence is also on the table. The score is strong because the point protects the debate from single-argument absolutism, but it does not solve the local argument from suffering by itself.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"49:27","role":"Draper-style symmetry","words":"Schmid says unknown goods may be cancelled by unknown worse evils, leaving first-order suffering evidence still counting against theism.","score":85,"critique":"Schmid's symmetry reply is a clean rebuttal to the skeptical-theist move. If there may be unknown goods connected to observed suffering, there may also be unknown worse evils or disvalues connected to the same world. Merely appealing to unseen goods does not automatically improve theism's probability unless there is reason to privilege favorable unknowns. The strength is that it preserves first-order evidence: visible suffering remains bad unless a specific defeater is supplied. The weakness is that the cancellation claim can be too quick if the theist has independent reasons to expect divine goodness. Even so, the point blocks mystery from functioning as an all-purpose shield. The score is high because the burden is kept symmetrical.","tags":[]}}]},{"title":"Goodness And Evil God","timebox":"50:28-56:25","score":{"pro":81,"con":82},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"time":"53:35","role":"Privation defense","words":"Horn says the evil-god challenge depends on treating good and evil as symmetric substances, not on a privation view of evil.","score":81,"critique":"Horn's privation reply is a substantive answer to Stephen Law's evil-god symmetry. If evil is not a competing positive substance but a privation of good, then a wholly evil necessary being may be incoherent in a way a wholly good God is not. That gives the theist a principled asymmetry rather than simply saying the evil-god hypothesis feels absurd. The weakness is that the reply inherits all the contested metaphysics of the privation theory. The panel cannot establish that account, and critics can ask whether horrendous suffering behaves like mere rust on metal rather than a positive experiential reality. The score is strong because the response is relevant, but its foundation remains disputed.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"51:52","role":"Evil-god symmetry","words":"O'Connor says a good God faces a problem of evil just as an evil God faces a problem of good.","score":82,"critique":"O'Connor's evil-god symmetry is a useful parity test. If theists dismiss an evil God because the world contains too much joy, order, and love, then atheists can ask why a good God is not similarly pressured by suffering. The argument is especially effective against ad hoc theodicies because equivalent reverse theodicies can be built for good under evil God. The weakness is that the symmetry depends on contested assumptions about good and evil. Classical theists can deny that evil is metaphysically parallel to good, and Horn does exactly that. The score is strong because the challenge exposes special pleading risks, but it is not decisive against privation-based theism.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"time":"55:54","role":"Metaphysical priority","words":"Horn says empirical observation alone cannot decide whether God is essentially good or evil; that is a prior metaphysical question.","score":79,"critique":"Horn's metaphysical-priority claim is coherent within classical theism. If God is identified with being or goodness itself, then divine goodness is not inferred by counting pleasant and painful events. That explains why he resists treating suffering as straightforward evidence that God is morally mixed or evil. The weakness is dialectical. The panel is asking whether the observed world makes perfect-being theism less plausible, and moving goodness into prior metaphysics risks insulating the doctrine from empirical pressure. A public argument still needs reasons to accept that metaphysical account. The score is solid because Horn clarifies his model, but the move can look protective if the model is not independently defended.","tags":[{"label":"Begging the question","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/","context":"Divine goodness is partly protected by prior metaphysical commitments that the critic does not yet share."}]},"con":{"time":"54:32","role":"Goodness burden","words":"O'Connor says Horn's own arguments aim to establish a God good by nature, so suffering still presses that conclusion.","score":83,"critique":"O'Connor's goodness-burden reply is well targeted. If the theist's arguments are not merely for a cause or unmoved mover but for the God of classical theism, then goodness is part of the conclusion being defended. The problem of evil cannot be set aside as only a challenge to divine character after the metaphysical argument has done its work. The weakness is that the reply does not defeat Horn's privation account; it says the account needs public defense before it can blunt evil evidence. That is enough for the local exchange. The score is high because O'Connor keeps the target fixed on perfect-being theism rather than generic first cause.","tags":[]}}]},{"title":"Parasitic Goods","timebox":"56:25-1:04:16","score":{"pro":79,"con":84},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"time":"57:34","role":"Defeated-evil vision","words":"Horn asks whether it is better for evil never to exist or for evil to be defeated and redeemed.","score":82,"critique":"Horn's defeated-evil vision is the most pastorally powerful theistic move. By invoking Les Miserables, restoration, and final compensation, he shifts the frame from suffering as a mere input cost to evil as something overcome within a larger story. That captures an important Christian intuition: redemption is not just pain plus arithmetic reward, but the transformation of tragedy. The weakness is that narrative beauty is not yet justification. The fact that a story of defeat and reunion is moving does not show that real racism, cancer, predation, or grief were worth permitting. The score is strong because the move explains Christianity's emotional force, but it needs stronger moral warrant.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"56:25","role":"Cancer-research analogy","words":"O'Connor says praising virtues born from suffering can sound like being glad cancer exists because cancer research is good.","score":84,"critique":"O'Connor's cancer-research analogy is one of the clearest attacks on parasitic-goods theodicy. It shows how strange it sounds to celebrate a good whose value depends on first allowing a grave evil. Cancer research, fundraising, and compassion are real goods, but most people would prefer no cancer and no need for those goods. The strength is that the analogy transfers directly to racism and bravery. The weakness is that theists can reply that some goods are not merely remedial utilities but components of a redeemed personal history. That reply needs work, but it prevents the analogy from covering every case. The score is high because the example makes the moral cost vivid.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"time":"1:03:24","role":"Infinite compensation","words":"Horn says infinite happiness after finite suffering may change intuitions about what God owes creatures.","score":76,"critique":"Horn's infinite-compensation point is relevant because afterlife claims do affect the moral accounting. If finite sufferers receive unending healing, justice, and joy, then some forms of temporal suffering may be judged differently than they would be under naturalism. That is an important internal Christian resource. The weakness is that compensation is not the same as permission. A victim's later restoration does not automatically justify causing or allowing the earlier harm, especially if the harm was avoidable. Infinite arithmetic can also obscure distributive and consent-related questions. The score is solid because the afterlife changes the evidential landscape, but lower because it risks treating persons as balances on a ledger.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"59:47","role":"Experience-machine reply","words":"O'Connor says Nozick's experience-machine intuition depends heavily on how the status quo is framed.","score":81,"critique":"O'Connor's experience-machine reply is more nuanced than simple hedonism. He argues that Nozick-style intuitions shift depending on whether one is asked to enter a machine or to leave one after already living there. That matters because theodicy often relies on intuitions about authenticity, reality, and whether painless happiness would be inferior. The weakness is that the discussion remains somewhat indirect. Status-quo effects may complicate experience-machine judgments, but they do not by themselves tell us whether a world with real suffering and redemption is better than a painless created order. The score is strong because O'Connor exposes a hidden framing effect, but the argument is a supporting pressure rather than the core evil case.","tags":[]}}]},{"title":"Antinatalism And Hope","timebox":"1:04:21-1:13:31","score":{"pro":82,"con":80},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"time":"1:08:11","role":"Creation analogy","words":"Horn says antinatalism debates about having children resemble theist replies about creating lives with goods and harms.","score":80,"critique":"Horn's antinatalism analogy is a clever late move. If many non-theists think it can be reasonable to have children despite foreseeable harms, then they already accept that bringing a vulnerable life into existence can be justified by goods, relationships, and the shape of a whole life. That makes some theistic creation replies less alien. The weakness is that the analogy has major limits. Human parents do not design biology, consent conditions, predation, disease, or eternal outcomes, while God would. They also lack omnipotent alternatives. The score is strong because the analogy reveals a shared moral structure, but it cannot carry the full creator-creature comparison morally.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"1:04:21","role":"Deontology challenge","words":"O'Connor says theodicy often assumes consequentialism even though many religious thinkers are otherwise deontological.","score":82,"critique":"O'Connor's deontology challenge is a useful reframing. Theodicies often talk as if enough good can outweigh suffering, yet many Christian moral theories reject simple consequentialist balancing. If God has duties to bring about free will, compassion, or courage, then the defense may be less about net utility and more about required goods. That clarifies what kind of moral theory the theist needs. The weakness is that O'Connor mostly opens a complication rather than developing it into a direct objection. Horn can answer that God's action is teleological without being crude consequentialism. The score is strong because the point exposes an unspoken moral framework, but it remains exploratory.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"time":"1:11:41","role":"Religious response","words":"Bertuzzi says religion may be a response to suffering, while Horn says Christianity is his hope for evil to be conquered.","score":84,"critique":"The closing religious-response move is powerful as existential explanation. Bertuzzi's suggestion that religion may be humanity's response to suffering, rather than merely a theory attacked by suffering, is insightful. Horn's appeal to Christianity as hope for reunion and evil's defeat also gives the theist side its strongest practical answer: removing God leaves the evil in place but removes the promised resolution. The weakness is evidential. A hope can be psychologically and morally important without being true, and O'Connor explicitly notes that religious ideas may arise because they answer human need. The score is high because the close captures Christianity's lived force, but it does not independently verify it.","tags":[]},"con":{"time":"1:13:21","role":"Wish-fulfillment reply","words":"O'Connor says he would love Christianity to be true, but that may explain why religious ideas exist in the first place.","score":78,"critique":"O'Connor's closing reply is humane and restrained. He does not sneer at Christian hope; he says he would love it to be true if he believed it, then notes that this desire may help explain why such religions arise. That is a fair psychological caution against confusing consolation with truth. The weakness is that the point is underdeveloped as an argument. Explaining why a belief is attractive does not show it is false, and human need can be a reason people notice a truth as well as a reason they invent one. The score is solid because the caution is apt, but it works as a closing caveat rather than a decisive rebuttal.","tags":[{"label":"Argument from ignorance","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/","context":"The possible origin of religious belief in human need is treated as cautionary without showing the belief false."}]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":78,"strengths":["Horn and Bertuzzi avoided easy answers, repeatedly distinguishing omnipotence, metaphysical possibility, theodicy, defense, and axiological starting points.","Their strongest replies treated evil as part of a broader worldview comparison rather than as a one-argument knockout.","They connected the abstract problem to Christian hope, final restoration, and the existential need for evil to be defeated."],"blunders":[{"text":"Their animal-suffering replies leaned on speculative compensation and uncertainty about animal experience while the scale of suffering remained vivid.","links":[{"label":"Scope neglect","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/scope-neglect/"}]},{"text":"Their skeptical-theist resources sometimes protected theism by widening unknown divine reasons without equally clear constraints.","links":[{"label":"Special pleading","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/"}]},{"text":"Their privation and divine-goodness replies depended on prior metaphysical commitments not established within the panel.","links":[{"label":"Begging the question","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"}]}]},"con":{"score":84,"strengths":["O'Connor and Schmid kept the central evidential burden clear: vast animal suffering is more expected on indifference than on perfect-being theism.","They pressed the cost of skeptical theism by showing how hidden reasons can also weaken natural theology and ordinary moral prediction.","Their higher-order-goods critiques used concrete examples, especially racism, cancer, and animal pain, to test whether the proposed goods really justify the evils."],"blunders":[{"text":"Their parasitic-goods objections sometimes relied on strong intuitive judgments about which possible worlds are better.","links":[{"label":"Subjective validation","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"}]},{"text":"Their evil-god symmetry challenge risked underplaying the metaphysical difference a privation theory makes if that theory is accepted.","links":[{"label":"Equivocation","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/"}]},{"text":"Their closing wish-fulfillment caution did not distinguish enough between a belief's psychological appeal and its truth value.","links":[{"label":"Argument from ignorance","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/"}]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman for Catholic-Protestant theodicy combines the side's strongest recorded claims into a measured case that directly answers the clearest objection raised in the debate.","premises":["The case's strongest foundation is this recorded point: Horn and Bertuzzi avoided easy answers, repeatedly distinguishing omnipotence, metaphysical possibility, theodicy, defense, and axiological starting points.","A second supporting consideration is this recorded point: Their strongest replies treated evil as part of a broader worldview comparison rather than as a one-argument knockout.","The cumulative case also gains support from this recorded point: They connected the abstract problem to Christian hope, final restoration, and the existential need for evil to be defeated.","The strengthened position must directly answer this recorded limitation: Their animal-suffering replies leaned on speculative compensation and uncertainty about animal experience while the scale of suffering remained vivid."],"conclusion":"Therefore, Catholic-Protestant theodicy is supported only insofar as this combined case explains the debate's central evidence better than its rival while surviving the stated limitation."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Comparative prediction test","text":"A stronger version would turn the motion into a comparison of expectations. The Catholic-Protestant theodicy side should identify which observations are more likely if its account is correct, which are more likely under the rival account, and what evidence would change the conclusion. Applied to the question—Does suffering, especially animal suffering and apparently gratuitous evil, make perfect-being theism improbable, or can theodicy, skeptical theism, higher goods, and Christian hope answer the challenge?—this approach replaces isolated possibility claims with a cumulative test and prevents one attractive example from carrying the entire case."},{"title":"Objection stress test","text":"The case can gain credibility by adopting its opponent's strongest pressure point: Their animal-suffering replies leaned on speculative compensation and uncertainty about animal experience while the scale of suffering remained vivid. Rather than dismissing that concern, the side should state a specific repair, acknowledge what remains uncertain, and describe how an observer could distinguish its explanation from the leading alternatives. A conclusion that survives this stress test is stronger because it shows where the argument could fail and why the available evidence still favors it."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman for Atheist-agnostic evil critique combines the side's strongest recorded claims into a measured case that directly answers the clearest objection raised in the debate.","premises":["The case's strongest foundation is this recorded point: O'Connor and Schmid kept the central evidential burden clear: vast animal suffering is more expected on indifference than on perfect-being theism.","A second supporting consideration is this recorded point: They pressed the cost of skeptical theism by showing how hidden reasons can also weaken natural theology and ordinary moral prediction.","The cumulative case also gains support from this recorded point: Their higher-order-goods critiques used concrete examples, especially racism, cancer, and animal pain, to test whether the proposed goods really justify the evils.","The strengthened position must directly answer this recorded limitation: Their parasitic-goods objections sometimes relied on strong intuitive judgments about which possible worlds are better."],"conclusion":"Therefore, Atheist-agnostic evil critique is supported only insofar as this combined case explains the debate's central evidence better than its rival while surviving the stated limitation."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Comparative prediction test","text":"A stronger version would turn the motion into a comparison of expectations. The Atheist-agnostic evil critique side should identify which observations are more likely if its account is correct, which are more likely under the rival account, and what evidence would change the conclusion. Applied to the question—Does suffering, especially animal suffering and apparently gratuitous evil, make perfect-being theism improbable, or can theodicy, skeptical theism, higher goods, and Christian hope answer the challenge?—this approach replaces isolated possibility claims with a cumulative test and prevents one attractive example from carrying the entire case."},{"title":"Objection stress test","text":"The case can gain credibility by adopting its opponent's strongest pressure point: Their parasitic-goods objections sometimes relied on strong intuitive judgments about which possible worlds are better. Rather than dismissing that concern, the side should state a specific repair, acknowledge what remains uncertain, and describe how an observer could distinguish its explanation from the leading alternatives. A conclusion that survives this stress test is stronger because it shows where the argument could fail and why the available evidence still favors it."}]}},"year":2022};
